title: Why tags have no exclusivity
tags: [[Hierarchical tags]]
kind: rationale
summary: Tags once could exclude their siblings, by a flag on the entries; it was removed in October 2026, being ambiguous once a tag has several parents and rarely what was meant.

Until October 2026 two `bool` fields on the tag entries made tags exclude one another:
`partition` on a parent (its direct children exclude one another) and `exclusive` on a
child (it excludes its siblings). `mf tag add` then dropped the excluded siblings, and
the classification flow stopped asking about them. The rule was data rather than code
because which tags go together depends on the vocabulary, not on the program.

It was removed (user, October 2026), for two reasons:

* ''Siblings stop being well defined'' once a tag may have several parents ([[Hierarchy references]]): a marathon under both `sport/courir` and `évènement` would be exclusive of the children of which parent? Every answer complicates subsumption, the scripts and the new type for a feature little used.
* ''An exclusive set is usually a number in disguise'': ratings `1` to `5` as five exclusive tags are an `int` field, which a query compares and sorts.

Flags written before stay as ordinary fields with no effect; nothing converts them.
`mf tag list` printed them as two more columns; it prints the path alone now, and the
tagging scripts ignore any column after it.
